% =!=> adds a hard cut to the tail of a hypothetical rule. Here `copy a b` is
% added while the rest of main runs; the cut means a later `copy a Z` commits
% to Z = b and does not also backtrack into the static rule `copy X X` (which
% would give Z = a). std.findall collects every solution and finds just one.
%
% With plain ==> this program would not compile: `copy` is a func, and adding
% `copy a b` next to the static rule is flagged as "mutual exclusion
% violated" -- exactly the choice point =!=> removes.

data tm.
symb a tm.
symb b tm.

func copy tm -> tm.
copy X X.

main :-
  copy a b =!=> std.findall (copy a Z_) Sols,
  print "solutions of copy a:" Sols.
